  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chance of showers and thunderstorms to arrive late \nSunday morning and Sunday afternoon.\n\nBroad ridging across the central Plains today, will continue to \nbuild, while a trough digs southeast across Quebec. Between these \ntwo features, a well defined thermal packing is set up across the \nGreat Lakes, where a corresponding warm front is strengthen across \nthe upper Great Lakes into New York. This being said, strong \nfrontogenetic forcing and ample moisture advection overhead the \nupper Great Lakes this afternoon through this evening, where \nmoisture and lift overhead the eastern Great Lakes are supporting \nfew showers across the area this afternoon.\n\nThe front will sag south across WNY this afternoon and evening where \nan area of showers associated with the front across Lake Erie \ntowards Michigan are pushing east into WNY as of 1330LT. This area \nof showers will continue to advect into WNY tonight, though the \nfrontal boundary will remain west of the Finger Lakes.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather will persist through much of the \nweek with occasional rounds of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nAforementioned strong ridge across the Central Plains Monday and \nTuesday will extend into the lower Ohio Valley while, troughing \nacross Quebec will continue across the eastern Great Lakes and \nNortheast. Meanwhile in the lower levels, a well defined thermal \ngradient will remain across the Great Lakes towards New York and \nPennsylvania through early next week. This boundary will waiver \nnorth/south at times as a few shortwave passages round the base of \nthe upper level trough.\n\nThe first shortwave will round the base of the trough Monday Monday \nnight , the frontal zone will meander northeast across the region \nwhich will result in the rain showers to move from the southwestern \nportions of the forecast area to the northeastern portions. \nInstability looks to be limited Monday, resulting in a slight chance \nof thunderstorms.\n\nThe pattern will become more conducive to strong to severe \nthunderstorms midweek,as a plume of an elevated mixed layer is \nforecast to slide northeastward across the Ohio Valley and possibly \nthe eastern Great Lakes. As this feature arrives, antecedent \nconditions will feature a warm and humid airmass, thus resulting in \nthe potential for moderate to strong instability as the shortwave \npasses by to the north. Additionally, with the passing of the \nshortwave, resultant flow will be modified to support increasing \nshear profiles. This all being said, the potential for severe \nweather midweek, however mesoscale details will continue to be \nmonitored in the coming days as the details for severe weather to \nbecome more clear.",
